WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.235 --> 00:00:07.094
- This one was our most voted-on
question in the pre-chat.

00:00:07.094 --> 00:00:11.011
People showed up early, racing
to get their questions in.

00:00:11.011 --> 00:00:13.011
It comes from Sebastian.

00:00:14.046 --> 00:00:15.785
"Are you doing the right thing

00:00:15.785 --> 00:00:18.810
if you need motivation
for what you're doing?"

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:20.041
- That's a great question.

00:00:20.041 --> 00:00:23.357
Do you know, um... Evan, I think,

00:00:23.357 --> 00:00:25.440
the answer is always yes.

00:00:26.585 --> 00:00:29.918
I kind of think it's almost,
like, and by the way, Evan,

00:00:29.918 --> 00:00:32.087
I think you're the world's
favorite broadcaster,

00:00:32.087 --> 00:00:35.130
that's the world's favorite
broadcaster, that's you.

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:36.631
You're that.

00:00:36.631 --> 00:00:38.435
You're way ahead of
people like James Corden,

00:00:38.435 --> 00:00:41.140
and stuff like that, who's an import.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:45.047
Um, I just think, what
I need to say here is

00:00:45.047 --> 00:00:49.217
it's almost like you need
to feed your inner self.

00:00:49.217 --> 00:00:52.836
And I could think of analogies,
obviously around nutrition,

00:00:52.836 --> 00:00:57.175
for health, exercise,
for fitness, well-being,

00:00:57.175 --> 00:01:01.387
but I do think you need
to, um, as an entrepreneur

00:01:01.387 --> 00:01:04.086
or business owner, you
need to create some people

00:01:04.086 --> 00:01:06.701
who will give you
positivity, and people who

00:01:06.701 --> 00:01:08.972
give you sort of challenge, uh,

00:01:08.972 --> 00:01:12.760
and really good, critical,
constructive input.

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:15.051
And it allows you to refine your ideas.

00:01:15.051 --> 00:01:18.004
And loads of ideas, and
I've done this, you know,

00:01:18.004 --> 00:01:21.352
we came up with an idea in the 1990s,

00:01:21.352 --> 00:01:23.336
and when we actually
launched it on the market

00:01:23.336 --> 00:01:25.384
it looked materially different from what

00:01:25.384 --> 00:01:28.159
our original idea was,
'cause we listened to people.

00:01:28.159 --> 00:01:31.628
We listened to customers,
they gave us great input.

00:01:31.628 --> 00:01:34.594
We never could imagine how
customers would have wanted

00:01:34.594 --> 00:01:38.025
to consume our product, and
that allowed us to improve it.

00:01:38.025 --> 00:01:41.891
So, I do think it's
important to get motivation,

00:01:41.891 --> 00:01:46.058
'cause I do think you need
to feed positivity, optimism,

00:01:47.856 --> 00:01:49.984
and also feed your idea,

00:01:49.984 --> 00:01:53.550
'cause otherwise I think you could lose

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:55.605
the will to pursue your dreams.

00:01:55.605 --> 00:01:58.153
So I definitely, yeah,
I would be an advocate

00:01:58.153 --> 00:02:02.360
of motivation, positivity,
and I do kind of see it

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:06.238
as almost like a food
you need to be consuming

00:02:06.238 --> 00:02:07.821
as an entrepreneur.

00:02:08.732 --> 00:02:10.572
- Yeah, I agree, I think we
all have moments of doubt.

00:02:10.572 --> 00:02:13.378
I don't think there's anybody
who never doubts themselves

00:02:13.378 --> 00:02:17.233
and always, you know, is
fearless 100% of the time.

00:02:17.233 --> 00:02:18.625
You need that in your life.

00:02:18.625 --> 00:02:20.548
What I would would examine, Sebastian,

00:02:20.548 --> 00:02:24.551
is if you are, I would
examine the motives.

00:02:24.551 --> 00:02:25.743
Like, why do you want to do this?

00:02:25.743 --> 00:02:27.356
Why is this business so important to you,

00:02:27.356 --> 00:02:29.075
whatever this thing is
that you're trying to do,

00:02:29.075 --> 00:02:30.943
why does it mean so much to you?

00:02:30.943 --> 00:02:34.057
And if it comes down to that
you don't love it anymore,

00:02:34.057 --> 00:02:35.748
or you're in it just
trying to make the money,

00:02:35.748 --> 00:02:38.665
then the motivation won't help you.

00:02:39.744 --> 00:02:41.938
You know, then it's time to
want to do something else

00:02:41.938 --> 00:02:44.615
that you can get all fired up by.

00:02:44.615 --> 00:02:48.268
But I think, like, for
me, it's a daily thing.

00:02:48.268 --> 00:02:51.580
I wake up and I need to
use my morning routine

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:54.957
to get me fired up for the day, right?

00:02:54.957 --> 00:02:58.368
Put on the music, do the
exercise in the morning,

00:02:58.368 --> 00:03:00.189
you know, like, I have my morning routine

00:03:00.189 --> 00:03:01.788
that gets me all set up,
and I need to do that

00:03:01.788 --> 00:03:04.474
every day, 'cause I have a
great talk with Stephen Kelly,

00:03:04.474 --> 00:03:07.407
guys, I'm like fired up for
the rest of the afternoon,

00:03:07.407 --> 00:03:08.487
and then I'm going to wake up the next day

00:03:08.487 --> 00:03:10.330
and I'm going to, like,
start from scratch again.

00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:13.766
And so, I need that
consistently in my schedule.

00:03:13.766 --> 00:03:15.546
But I think if you find
that you don't love

00:03:15.546 --> 00:03:17.003
what you're doing anymore,
then it's time to move on

00:03:17.003 --> 00:03:19.003
and find something else.

00:03:20.082 --> 00:03:22.005
Stephen, you have a crazy morning routine,

00:03:22.005 --> 00:03:24.881
you're up, and like,
you're running, and...

00:03:24.881 --> 00:03:27.341
- Yeah, I love, I try and do, particularly

00:03:27.341 --> 00:03:29.557
I've done a lot of traveling,
we do these kick-offs

00:03:29.557 --> 00:03:32.076
around, see all our
colleagues, so we did, like,

00:03:32.076 --> 00:03:36.692
one week down in Johannesburg,
from London to Pretoria,

00:03:36.692 --> 00:03:40.109
up to Paris the next day, on the Tuesday.

00:03:41.268 --> 00:03:44.298
I think then we went
down to Madrid Wednesday,

00:03:44.298 --> 00:03:46.914
up to a place in the north
of England called Newcastle,

00:03:46.914 --> 00:03:49.276
and then Friday we
finished up in Frankfurt.

00:03:49.276 --> 00:03:52.373
So, I'll be honest, it
is full-on, it's intense,

00:03:52.373 --> 00:03:54.966
it's like from 5 o'clock in the morning

00:03:54.966 --> 00:03:58.540
til probably midnight, and if I don't try,

00:03:58.540 --> 00:03:59.932
on most of these things, if I can,

00:03:59.932 --> 00:04:01.711
sometimes it's just not possible,

00:04:01.711 --> 00:04:05.169
but if I get up and do, like,
even a 20 or 30 minute run,

00:04:05.169 --> 00:04:06.877
on that one it was great fun, actually,

00:04:06.877 --> 00:04:08.705
'cause we invited, 'cause we'd
been doing loads of things

00:04:08.705 --> 00:04:10.112
through the Sage Foundation, which is

00:04:10.112 --> 00:04:14.039
the charitable arm of
Sage, and we did challenge

00:04:14.039 --> 00:04:17.321
all our colleagues to
chase and beat the CEO.

00:04:17.321 --> 00:04:20.408
So they all came out, you
know, down to Johannesburg,

00:04:20.408 --> 00:04:23.210
we went on a run together,
with a load of colleagues.

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:24.955
Paris was, oh, mindblowing!

00:04:24.955 --> 00:04:27.958
I got up, like, some crazy
time, like 5 o'clock,

00:04:27.958 --> 00:04:30.006
and outside the hotel was, I don't know,

00:04:30.006 --> 00:04:31.913
must have been 30, 40 colleagues,

00:04:31.913 --> 00:04:34.162
and loads of these guys, amazing,

00:04:34.162 --> 00:04:36.212
what sacrifice for them,
getting up even an hour early,

00:04:36.212 --> 00:04:38.429
coming in from the suburbs, but we ran

00:04:38.429 --> 00:04:39.630
from this hotel, it's like, probably,

00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:41.922
about 5K, it's quite a long way,

00:04:41.922 --> 00:04:46.852
uphill up to the amazing
church called Sacré-Couer,

00:04:46.852 --> 00:04:49.713
it's on top of the hill in Paris,

00:04:49.713 --> 00:04:52.921
and you were overlooking
the whole of Paris itself.

00:04:52.921 --> 00:04:56.379
The sun started rising,
and it's magical, you know?

00:04:56.379 --> 00:04:57.353
But, why do I do it?

00:04:57.353 --> 00:05:00.860
Um, it lifts me, it gives me energy,

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:03.824
and there's loads of physical
evidence about endorphins

00:05:03.824 --> 00:05:05.613
and all this sort of stuff.

00:05:05.613 --> 00:05:08.980
So it is good, and I just
think, actually, Evan,

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:12.283
and I think, pfft, probably
one of my, sort of,

00:05:12.283 --> 00:05:15.064
self-criticisms, I don't
know whether it's OCD or....

00:05:15.064 --> 00:05:18.472
I'm pretty, sort of, extreme,
I love a routine, you know?

00:05:18.472 --> 00:05:20.438
I kind of like a routine.

00:05:20.438 --> 00:05:24.643
So the same routine sort of
helps me get into the zone

00:05:24.643 --> 00:05:28.414
of coming in the office,
having energy, you know,

00:05:28.414 --> 00:05:30.667
tackling good days and bad days, really.

00:05:30.667 --> 00:05:33.430
Some days are good,
some days are less good.

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:36.080
But it gives me a good start to the day.

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:39.104
So, and I think, you know,
if you start the day well

00:05:39.104 --> 00:05:42.138
then it's a great start
to the week, and literally

00:05:42.138 --> 00:05:44.432
it gets you off to a right place.

00:05:44.432 --> 00:05:47.922
And if you do, you know,
if you've had a weekend,

00:05:47.922 --> 00:05:50.183
it's funny when you have weekends,
'cause you kind of reflect,

00:05:50.183 --> 00:05:51.478
it's like holidays, vacations.

00:05:51.478 --> 00:05:52.544
Let's talk about this.

00:05:52.544 --> 00:05:55.347
Just a completely, thing
that came into my mind.

00:05:55.347 --> 00:05:58.877
Loads of people on vacation
will think, "You know,

00:05:58.877 --> 00:06:01.477
reassessing priorities, do
I really want to do this?

00:06:01.477 --> 00:06:03.868
I've been at this business two years,

00:06:03.868 --> 00:06:05.812
am I really going to break through?

00:06:05.812 --> 00:06:07.506
Is it all too hard?

00:06:07.506 --> 00:06:11.023
I'm sitting here drinking
tequila slammers in some

00:06:11.023 --> 00:06:14.023
exotic resort in Hawaii, you know?"

00:06:14.023 --> 00:06:18.403
Ugh! And little voices,
I find, on vacation,

00:06:18.403 --> 00:06:21.881
really get into you, or
you can kind of turn it

00:06:21.881 --> 00:06:24.126
and say, "Actually, it's
a great time to reflect,

00:06:24.126 --> 00:06:26.526
and some of the things I'm
doing with the business

00:06:26.526 --> 00:06:29.291
work in terms of my idea, but some of them

00:06:29.291 --> 00:06:30.466
I could do differently."

00:06:30.466 --> 00:06:34.332
And some people come back from
vacation hugely energized,

00:06:34.332 --> 00:06:38.585
but also being able to
reflect from the outside in

00:06:38.585 --> 00:06:40.710
on what wasn't going
well, and then come back

00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:42.442
straight in with the energy to fix it.

00:06:42.442 --> 00:06:45.931
So it is a good time to, if
you can do that, and I know

00:06:45.931 --> 00:06:48.510
loads of people do things
like, honestly, mindfulness,

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:51.436
meditation, I think, you
know, I applaud those people,

00:06:51.436 --> 00:06:54.183
'cause it just gives
you the space to think

00:06:54.183 --> 00:06:58.279
and reflect on what's
working well and what's not,

00:06:58.279 --> 00:07:00.709
and, you know, to reconnect
with your passion.

00:07:00.709 --> 00:07:01.739
So I love that.

00:07:01.739 --> 00:07:03.514
Hey, Evan, do you do any of those things

00:07:03.514 --> 00:07:05.721
to help you get in the zone?

00:07:05.721 --> 00:07:08.221
- Uh, so for vacations, I, uh,

00:07:09.488 --> 00:07:11.611
I hate beach vacations.

00:07:11.611 --> 00:07:14.021
I could do, maybe, like,
half a day at the beach

00:07:14.021 --> 00:07:16.936
and then I'm out, then I
got to go see the city,

00:07:16.936 --> 00:07:18.555
or I'm looking at the restaurant thinking,

00:07:18.555 --> 00:07:20.215
"How could this business be better?"

00:07:20.215 --> 00:07:23.786
Or, you know, analyzing the
people selling the floats.

00:07:23.786 --> 00:07:27.953
And anyway, it's like, I,
uh, I need the stimulation.

00:07:28.964 --> 00:07:31.340
In terms of a morning routine, though,

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:34.389
I wake up, like, I watch
the videos on my channel.

00:07:34.389 --> 00:07:36.526
I made the channel, selfishly, for myself.

00:07:36.526 --> 00:07:39.642
The famous entrepreneurs that
we profile was for myself,

00:07:39.642 --> 00:07:41.991
because I wanted to be
around these great thinkers

00:07:41.991 --> 00:07:44.943
and have some of their
mindset kind of seep into me.

00:07:44.943 --> 00:07:47.929
And uh, it was a selfish pursuit
that I shared with others,

00:07:47.929 --> 00:07:50.873
and they're enjoying it as well.

00:07:50.873 --> 00:07:52.672
And I find that I need
that on a regular...

00:07:52.672 --> 00:07:53.522
Look at my wall!

00:07:53.522 --> 00:07:55.657
Like, I walk into this
room every day, right?

00:07:55.657 --> 00:07:58.626
We've got people that I
look up to and I learn from

00:07:58.626 --> 00:08:00.820
on my wall here, and it's all part,

00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:04.029
I think everybody needs
to design an environment

00:08:04.029 --> 00:08:06.235
and a schedule that gets
them fired up, you know?

00:08:06.235 --> 00:08:08.577
Like, you may not know who this guys is,

00:08:08.577 --> 00:08:10.721
or those people are,
like, it doesn't matter,

00:08:10.721 --> 00:08:12.884
they mean something to me.

00:08:12.884 --> 00:08:15.639
So it's not, like, copy mine
and put it up on your wall.

00:08:15.639 --> 00:08:17.689
Find the people that inspire you.

00:08:17.689 --> 00:08:19.166
I have these people
looking at me when I walk

00:08:19.166 --> 00:08:21.756
into the office, it gets me fired up to go

00:08:21.756 --> 00:08:23.900
and do the things that I
want to do, and kind of

00:08:23.900 --> 00:08:26.845
think a little bit bigger and
get out of the comfort zone.

00:08:26.845 --> 00:08:31.243
Um, so... now you're traveling
in your different city

00:08:31.243 --> 00:08:34.639
every time we talk,
and, you know, but, uh,

00:08:34.639 --> 00:08:36.082
for those of you who are not jet-setting

00:08:36.082 --> 00:08:37.921
around the world, you know,
you could do something

00:08:37.921 --> 00:08:39.414
in your home office, right?

00:08:39.414 --> 00:08:40.893
Just have something up on the wall

00:08:40.893 --> 00:08:42.755
that means something to you.

00:08:42.755 --> 00:08:45.329
- Make it special for you,
just, I think it's great

00:08:45.329 --> 00:08:47.729
what you do, Evan, and
I think, what I would

00:08:47.729 --> 00:08:50.529
encourage us all to do
is kind of reconnect us

00:08:50.529 --> 00:08:53.394
with our dream, and whether it's pictures,

00:08:53.394 --> 00:08:56.850
or imagery, or prose,
you know, to connect us

00:08:56.850 --> 00:08:59.132
with why we started our business,

00:08:59.132 --> 00:09:01.590
and what our big idea could
do to change the world.

00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:04.176
And, uh, if you come
and see that every day

00:09:04.176 --> 00:09:06.593
I think that gives you power.

